1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,393
Your release looks good. But then you roll it back, and a customer's new order disappears.

2
00:00:06,043 --> 00:00:11,792
Built by Shivam Gupta, UndoProof catches that failure before a database migration ships.

3
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,129
Here's our checkout example. The new release works.

4
00:00:19,129 --> 00:00:21,458
The trouble starts when we try to undo it.

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,691
This contract lists the migration, the application queries, and the data we need to protect.

6
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:36,037
Look at this rollback. It drops today's orders table and restores a snapshot from before the release.

7
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,359
Now, a customer places order one hundred and four.

8
00:00:42,359 --> 00:00:45,113
It's new data. The rollback needs to keep it.

9
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,938
Let's run the rehearsal. This is real Postgres, running through PGlite.

10
00:00:51,938 --> 00:00:54,029
And the release plan is blocked.

11
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:58,632
The forward tests passed. The rollback SQL worked, too.

12
00:00:58,632 --> 00:01:02,570
But look at data preservation. That's where the failure shows up.

13
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:07,473
Before rollback, four orders. After rollback, three.

14
00:01:07,473 --> 00:01:10,096
Order one hundred and four has disappeared.

15
00:01:10,746 --> 00:01:13,958
We compare the actual values in the data invariant.

16
00:01:13,958 --> 00:01:17,485
A successful SQL command doesn't hide the missing order.

17
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:25,146
This handoff gives Bob the exact contract and the failure evidence.

18
00:01:25,146 --> 00:01:28,690
The developer opens it in Bob IDE to investigate.

19
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,735
Here's a candidate repair we can review.

20
00:01:31,735 --> 00:01:35,813
It keeps the compatible new column when the application rolls back.

21
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:41,578
We remove the destructive snapshot restore. The customer's order stays in the database.

22
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,897
Apply the reviewed change, then run the same checks again.

23
00:01:45,897 --> 00:01:47,758
This time, every check passes.

24
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,496
All four orders survive. The old application queries still work, too.

25
00:01:57,146 --> 00:02:02,370
We can roll back the application without destroying the compatible database changes.

26
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:09,397
Export the evidence for the release review: the tested contract, executed SQL,

27
00:02:09,397 --> 00:02:11,292
row comparisons, and runtime.

28
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:15,349
History keeps the failed run and the repaired run.

29
00:02:15,349 --> 00:02:20,226
Bring your own contracts, or use the same engine as a command-line release gate.

30
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,389
Here is Bob working directly in this repository.

31
00:02:25,389 --> 00:02:30,794
It added forty-one regression tests for source-derived checkout contracts, including

32
00:02:30,794 --> 00:02:33,755
the lost order and unchanged repair invariant.

33
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:39,071
We reran the tests. The code and reviewed findings are in the repository.

34
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:47,687
UndoProof is open source. Next, we'll test shared release policies and retained

35
00:02:47,687 --> 00:02:49,869
evidence with engineering teams.

36
00:02:50,518 --> 00:02:53,078
Results cover the tested SQL and fixtures.

37
00:02:53,078 --> 00:02:55,700
The way back belongs in the release review.
